        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          STS-102, also called International Space Station (ISS) assembly flight 5A.1, was the eighth Space Shuttle mission supporting assembly of the ISS.  The Shuttle launched on March 8, 2001, carrying seven crewmembers, Commander James Wetherbee, Pilot James Kelly, Mission Specialist Andrew Thomas, Mission Specialist Paul Richards, and the Expedition Two crew, Commander Yury Usachev, and Flight Engineers Jim Voss and Susan Helms.  In the payload bay, the ISS Launch Package 5A.1 included the Leonardo Multi-Purpose Logistics Module (MPLM), the Integrated Cargo Carrier (ICC), two Assembly Power Converter Units (APCUs), and the Structural Dynamics Model Validation.  In-cabin payloads included the Shuttle Automatic Reboost Tuning, Single String Global Positioning System, and Crosswind Landing Performance.  Space Shuttle Discovery also provided transportation back to Earth for the first resident ISS crew.
<P>
STS-102 docked with the ISS on March 9, 2001 at 11:33 p.m. CST.  The primary objective was to berth the Italian-built Leonardo Multipurpose Logistics Module to the Station.  Two space walks were conducted to complete the assembly operations.  
<P>
The Leonardo Multipurpose Logistics Module is the first of three such pressurized modules that will serve as the International Space Station's "moving vans," carrying laboratory racks filled with equipment, experiments and supplies to and from the station aboard the space shuttle. The unpiloted, reusable logistics modules function as both a cargo carrier and a space station module when they are flown. Mounted in the space shuttle's cargo bay for launch and landing, they are berthed to the station using the shuttle's robotic arm after the shuttle has docked. While berthed to the station, racks of equipment are unloaded from the module and then old racks and equipment are reloaded and returned to Earth. The logistics module is then detached from the station and positioned back into the shuttle's cargo bay for the trip home. When in the cargo bay, the cargo module is independent of the shuttle cabin, and there is no passageway for shuttle crewmembers to travel from the shuttle cabin to the module. To function as an attached station module, as well as a cargo transport, the logistics modules include components that provide some life support, fire detection and suppression, electrical distribution and computer functions. Eventually, the modules will also carry refrigerator freezers for transporting experiment samples and food to and from the station. Although built in Italy, the logistics modules, technically known as Multipurpose Logistics Modules or MPLMs, are owned by the U.S. and provided in exchange for Italian access to US research time on the station.
<P>
Over the course of joint operations between the station and shuttle crews, Discovery crewmembers worked with the station crew unloading almost five tons of experiments and equipment from Leonardo and packing almost one ton of items for return to Earth. Discovery's crew also set the stage for continued expansion of the station by installing a platform that will be used to mount a Canadian-built robotic arm to the station. 
<P>
STS-102 undocked from the ISS on March 17, 2001 at 10:31 p.m. CST.  Discovery landed on March 20 at Kennedy Space Center (KSC) in Florida returning the Expedition One crew (Bill Shepherd, Sergei Krikalev and Yuri Gidzenko).  This was the 17th night landing.  The Expedition Two crew will return on STS-105, ISS assembly flight 7A.1.
